<p style="position: absolute; left: 67px; top: 166px; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; color: #4A4542">tap a place. i’ll tell you why.</p>

<p style="position: absolute; left: 67px; top: 858px; width: 625px; margin: 0; font-family: 'Hanken Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.5; color: #6f6862">if you liked how i described these places, imagine what i’d write about yours. <a href="mailto:kritikaupadhyay2001@gmail.com" style="font-size: 13px; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; white-space: nowrap">let’s talk</a></p>

<article style="position: absolute; left: 820px; top: 64px; width: 628px; height: 854px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; font-family: 'EB Garamond', serif; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.5; color: #2a2724; text-align: justify; hyphens: auto">
<img src="/coffee/cafe.jpg" alt="the coffee counter at a cafe, chalkboard menus and warm pendant lights" style="display: block; flex: none; width: 628px; height: 380px; object-fit: cover">
<h2 style="margin: 22px 0 16px; font-family: 'EB Garamond', serif; font-style: italic; font-weight: 400; font-size: 32px; line-height: 1.1; text-align: left; color: #1C1B1F">i like to hangout alone at a crowded cafe. <span style="color: #A9452A">but why?</span></h2>
<div style="flex: none; column-count: 2; column-gap: 56px">

<p style="margin: 0 0 12px">if you live in bangalore, you’ve noticed how much people love to work out of cafés. someone once told me it all started here. i believed it instantly. even founders must get bored of their own company, <em>i thought to myself.</em></p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 12px">so i became one of those people too. it was hard at first. i would get distracted easily. <em>i like to read a room. any room.</em> now i can read a book in peace, whatever’s happening around me.</p>
<p style="margin: -6px 0 12px; font-family: 'EB Garamond', serif; font-style: italic; color: #A9452A; text-align: left; font-size: 20px">that’s when i started wondering why we do this at all.</p>

<p style="margin: 0 0 12px">it isn’t always quiet. at kohe in hyderabad, people bustle in and out. friends arguing, young parents trying to have a life, families home after years abroad. i spot more stories there than i could ever write.</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 12px">but i have come to realise that Bangalore is a city of choices. and people here live like an adult. could that be why, hanging out at cafes alone caught on. it's a choice to be alone and not owe an answer to anyone. <span style="color: #A9452A">what do you think?</span></p>

</div>
